WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.069
How long is it? How heavy is
it? How much does it hold?

2
00:00:08.289 --> 00:00:12.886
Each question has its own tool,
its own units and its own scale.

3
00:00:13.386 --> 00:00:16.544
Length is how far it is
from one end to the other.

4
00:00:16.764 --> 00:00:21.362
A ruler measures it. This pencil
runs from zero to fifteen.

5
00:00:21.582 --> 00:00:24.322
So the pencil is fifteen centimetres long.

6
00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:29.697
A hundred centimetres make one metre.
A thousand metres make one kilometre.

7
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:34.375
A door is about two metres. The
next town is five kilometres away.

8
00:00:34.875 --> 00:00:38.311
Mass is how heavy a thing
is. You weigh it on a scale.

9
00:00:38.531 --> 00:00:41.968
A thousand grams balance
one kilogram exactly.

10
00:00:42.188 --> 00:00:46.739
An apple is about a hundred grams.
A bag of rice is two kilograms.

11
00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:51.371
Big does not mean heavy. A bag of
feathers can weigh less than a stone.

12
00:00:51.871 --> 00:00:54.611
Capacity is how much a container holds.

13
00:00:54.831 --> 00:00:57.617
A thousand millilitres fill one litre.

14
00:00:57.837 --> 00:01:03.317
A teaspoon holds about five millilitres.
A mug of tea holds three hundred.

15
00:01:03.537 --> 00:01:07.392
So half a litre is five
hundred millilitres, not fifty.

16
00:01:07.892 --> 00:01:13.000
A scale is the marked line on an
instrument. What is this pointer reading?

17
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.192
Work out what one small
mark is worth first.

18
00:01:16.412 --> 00:01:19.756
Three hundred and four hundred
are printed, a hundred apart.

19
00:01:19.976 --> 00:01:23.459
Count the gaps between them,
not the marks. There are two.

20
00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:29.205
A hundred shared between two gaps is fifty.
The pointer reads three hundred and fifty.

21
00:01:29.705 --> 00:01:32.166
A ruler does not have to start at zero.

22
00:01:32.386 --> 00:01:35.498
This pencil begins at the
two and ends at the nine.

23
00:01:35.718 --> 00:01:39.480
Read only the end and you
get nine. That is too long.

24
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.207
Count on from the two, not the zero.

25
00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:46.468
Count on, one centimetre at a
time: seven centimetres long.

26
00:01:46.968 --> 00:01:50.543
A right angle is a square
corner. It is a quarter turn.

27
00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:54.850
The corner of a book is one. Hold
it against any angle to check.

28
00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:58.925
This angle opens less, so it
is smaller than a right angle.

29
00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:04.996
This one opens wider, so it is bigger.
Long arms do not make a bigger angle.

30
00:02:05.216 --> 00:02:09.396
Two right angles side by side
make a straight line, a half turn.

31
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:14.029
Length in centimetres, mass in
grams, capacity in millilitres.

32
00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:17.639
Work out what one mark is worth
before you read any scale.

33
00:02:17.859 --> 00:02:21.574
Count on from the start when
a ruler starts past zero.

34
00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:26.020
A right angle is a quarter turn, and
two of them make a straight line.
